  'body'        => <<<'HTML'
<p>The ideal home for a family of four in Texas balances togetherness with privacy, handles the heat, and has room to grow as kids get older. Style matters, but layout, bedroom count and storage matter more. Below are the home styles Texas families build most often, how each one works for four people, and the layout decisions that make the biggest difference.</p>

<h2>Start with how your family lives</h2>
<p>Before you fall for an elevation, answer a few practical questions:</p>
<ul>
<li>How old are the kids now, and what will they need in ten years?</li>
<li>Does anyone work from home and need a quiet office with a door?</li>
<li>Do grandparents visit often or might they move in someday?</li>
<li>Do you entertain indoors, outdoors or both?</li>
<li>How big is your lot, and are there HOA style rules?</li>
</ul>
<p>Your answers point to the right style and size much faster than browsing photos. Our guide on <a href="/how-to-choose-a-floor-plan-for-your-new-home/">how to choose a floor plan</a> has a full worksheet.</p>

<h2>Popular Texas home styles for a family of four</h2>
<h3>The ranch-style home</h3>
<p>The quintessential Texas house: single-story, long and low, with an open living, dining and kitchen area and often a deep front porch. Everything on one level makes it easy with toddlers and comfortable as you age. The trade-off is a larger foundation and roof per square foot, and it needs a wider lot.</p>
<h3>The two-story traditional or colonial</h3>
<p>Common areas downstairs, bedrooms upstairs. That separation gives parents quiet when kids are asleep and keeps guests away from private spaces. Two-story homes fit narrower suburban lots and can cost less per square foot because the foundation and roof are smaller. Consider a primary suite on the ground floor if you plan to stay long-term.</p>
<h3>The modern farmhouse</h3>
<p>Rustic charm with modern function: open floor plans, big kitchens, mudrooms and wrap-around porches. It suits families who want a casual, durable look. Metal roofs, a popular farmhouse feature, also reflect Texas sun well.</p>
<h3>The Mediterranean or Spanish-style home</h3>
<p>Stucco walls, tile roofs and courtyards are a natural fit for South and Central Texas. Courtyards and covered patios create shaded outdoor rooms where kids can play and adults can gather. Stucco needs proper detailing to manage moisture, so choose an experienced installer.</p>
<h3>The Texas Hill Country home</h3>
<p>Limestone, wood and metal, with deep porches and big windows framing the view. Durable materials hold up to kids and weather, and the style is popular for resale in Central Texas. Stone can add cost, so some families use it on the front elevation and simpler materials elsewhere.</p>
<h3>Contemporary and modern</h3>
<p>Clean lines, large windows and flexible open spaces. Modern homes can be very efficient if glass is placed carefully; big west-facing windows without shading will fight your AC all summer.</p>

<h2>Style comparison at a glance</h2>
<table>
<thead><tr><th>Style</th><th>Best for</th><th>Watch out for</th></tr></thead>
<tbody>
<tr><td>Ranch</td><td>Young kids, aging in place, wide lots</td><td>Bigger foundation and roof; longer hallways</td></tr>
<tr><td>Two-story</td><td>Narrow lots, privacy between kids and adults</td><td>Stairs; laundry location; upstairs cooling</td></tr>
<tr><td>Modern farmhouse</td><td>Casual living, big kitchens</td><td>Trend-driven finishes that may date</td></tr>
<tr><td>Mediterranean</td><td>Courtyards and outdoor living</td><td>Stucco detailing and maintenance</td></tr>
<tr><td>Hill Country</td><td>Durability, Central Texas resale</td><td>Cost of stone</td></tr>
<tr><td>Contemporary</td><td>Light-filled, flexible spaces</td><td>Heat gain from large glass</td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>

<h2>Layout features that matter most for four people</h2>
<ol>
<li><strong>Bedrooms and baths.</strong> Three bedrooms is a minimum; four, or three plus a flex room, gives breathing room. A Jack-and-Jill or second full bath for the kids eases morning rushes.</li>
<li><strong>Split or grouped bedrooms.</strong> With babies, many parents want kids nearby. With teens, a split plan that puts the primary suite on the opposite side of the house gives everyone privacy.</li>
<li><strong>Mudroom and drop zone.</strong> A bench, hooks and cubbies by the garage door keep backpacks and cleats out of the kitchen.</li>
<li><strong>Open but zoned living.</strong> An open kitchen and family room for togetherness, plus a separate room for homework, a home office or a playroom that can close off noise.</li>
<li><strong>Storage everywhere.</strong> Walk-in pantry, linen closets, garage storage and closets that are actually deep enough.</li>
<li><strong>Covered outdoor space.</strong> A shaded patio where kids can play and adults can eat outside most of the year.</li>
</ol>
<div class="callout"><p><strong>Pro tip:</strong> Tape out key rooms at full size on the slab or in a parking lot before you finalize the plan. Seeing a 12 by 12 bedroom or a 4-foot hallway in real life tells you more than a drawing ever will.</p></div>
<p>A [[amz:tape_measure|sturdy tape measure]] and a [[amz:laser_measure|laser measure]] make this quick, and they are useful all through the build.</p>

<h2>Build in safety and practicality for kids</h2>
<p>New construction is the easiest time to get family safety right:</p>
<ul>
<li>Interconnected smoke and carbon monoxide alarms, as code typically requires; confirm details with your permitting office</li>
<li>Tamper-resistant outlets, which modern codes generally require in homes</li>
<li>Durable flooring such as tile or luxury vinyl plank in high-traffic areas</li>
<li>Window placement and locks with child safety in mind, especially upstairs</li>
<li>A smart lock or keypad so kids never need a key</li>
</ul>
<!--products:smoke_co,smart_lock,video_doorbell|Family safety gear to plan for in a new build-->

<h2>Plan for the years ahead</h2>
<p>Kids grow fast. The nursery becomes a homework room, then a teenager's retreat, then a guest room when they leave for college. Design a few spaces to change roles easily: a flex room with a closet that can become a bedroom, a bonus room that works as a playroom now and a media room later, and a ground-floor bedroom near a full bath that could serve a visiting grandparent. Our guide on <a href="/how-to-choose-a-floor-plan-for-your-new-home/">choosing a floor plan</a> has more ideas for flexible layouts, and the <a href="/new-home-construction-timeline/">construction timeline</a> shows when each decision needs to be locked in.</p>

<h2>Next steps</h2>
